WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:09.980
Even if you've never tried a bag

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.370
of Flamin' Hot Cheetos,

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:12.900
you probably know they exist.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.200
And you probably know
they're extremely spicy.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.110
The fan culture around
Flamin' Hot Cheetos has

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:18.770
helped these fiery cheese puffs

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.570
gain widespread notoriety,
survived school bans,

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.210
media panic, and an origin story debacle.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:27.190
Fans have imbued meaning and
value into Flamin' Hot Cheetos

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:29.360
and they've become
ingrained in pop culture.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.570
Food nannies looking to
snatch them from our hands

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:32.820
don't stand a chance.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.030
They're the outlaw snack
that may be more American

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:36.080
than apple pie.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.500
My name is Tirsa Farah and I
am the owner, executive chef,

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.480
along with my husband,
of Tirsa's Mexican Cafe.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.400
And we are, in what I
call, the living room of

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.190
my little casita Mexicana restaurant in

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.750
downtown Los Angeles, Chinatown, Downtown,

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:54.440
whatever you guys want to call it.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:55.600
Tirsa Farrah started her cafe

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.310
because she wanted to be her own boss.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:59.460
Her menu is inspired by her experience

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.920
growing up in Los Angeles.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.520
An L.A. girl with Mexican roots.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.580
You know, so that's what you're
going to find here, is my,

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.160
my take on the L.A. flavors
and the Mexican scene growing

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.230
up in a house that's literally divided.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:13.220
My dad was born in Mexico and my mom

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.240
is an L.A. native, herself.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.440
One of the ingredients in

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.470
Farah's dishes, Flamin' Hot Cheetos,

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:20.570
which she sprinkles on a variety of dishes

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.490
from sopas, to nachos, to esquites.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.530
She first encountered
the snack in high school.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.780
Either before school or after school,

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.680
you were gonna go get a bag of chips.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.010
And the only one you were
going to get was "Flamin' Hot."

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:35.030
It's just kind of this kooky tradition

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.230
even though we're like,
why are we eating this?

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:38.560
We just can't stop.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:40.540
Part of the lore around
Flamin' Hot Cheetos

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:43.400
was the dynamic story of their
self-proclaimed inventor,

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.690
Richard Montañez.

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:48.857
What would happen if I put chile

00:01:48.857 --> 00:01:51.872
on a Cheeto?

00:01:51.872 --> 00:01:55.230
Montañez said he was working as a janitor at Frito-Lay
when he said he dreamed up

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.440
the idea of a spicy
version of the company's

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:58.960
famous cheese puffs.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.110
That led to a meeting with

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:02.620
Frito-Lay's president,
and that impulse decision

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:04.860
would become a billion-dollar snack.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.130
The story went viral.

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:09.220
Montañez, a Mexican-American,
earned legend status

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:11.140
and was invited to give
motivational speeches

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:14.110
at corporate events for
Walmart, Target, and Harvard.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.630
He became a hero in the
Mexican-American community

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.550
and his rise was considered an example  

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.280
of the American dream fulfilled.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.180
Journalists repeated the
story without bothering

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:24.120
to investigate its veracity.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.030
Montañez chronicled his
story in a 2021 book.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.670
And it's even being made into a movie by

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:31.030
actress and producer, Eva Longoria.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:33.450
Turns out that story probably isn't true.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:35.050
The times quoted several former

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:38.230
Frito-Lay employees as
saying he had nothing to do

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.580
with Flamin' Hot Cheetos and
that the product was created

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.742
by a team in 1989, based out of Texas.

00:02:44.742 --> 00:02:46.810
Montañez started taking
credit for their creation

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:49.760
in the early two thousands,
almost two decades after

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:50.830
they were invented.

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:52.730
Montañez and his fans still maintained

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:54.990
that he was the true inventor.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:59.833
The best way to destroy a positive message

00:02:59.833 --> 00:03:01.964
is to destroy the messenger.

00:03:01.964 --> 00:03:04.687
Never allow that to happen.

00:03:04.687 --> 00:03:07.709
So what if the Flamin' Hot Cheetos origin story is a fairytale?

00:03:07.709 --> 00:03:09.259
It's part of the folklore.

00:03:09.259 --> 00:03:11.380
What's most interesting about
these spicy cheese puffs

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:14.119
aren't what actually
happened at Frito-Lay,

00:03:14.119 --> 00:03:16.250
but how consumers
re-imagined a corporate snack

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:20.276
with a Mexican twist into
something uniquely American.

00:03:20.276 --> 00:03:21.810
Flamin' Hot Cheetos are in rap songs,

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:24.920
YouTube and TikTok videos,
clothing and jewelry,

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:27.730
and a variety of dishes with
roots all over the world.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.070
Even Katy Perry wore a
Flamin' Hot Cheeto outfit

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:32.809
with matching purse to a Halloween party.

00:03:32.809 --> 00:03:34.260
The fan culture around Flamin' Hot Cheetos

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.940
has been perplexing to food nannies

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:37.630
intent on controlling our consumption

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:41.531
of artificial dyes and
high-calorie cornmeal.

00:03:41.531 --> 00:03:42.767
Flamin' Hot Cheetos, anyone?

00:03:42.767 --> 00:03:44.040
Flamin' Hot Cheetos.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.747
So hot, careful where you eat 'em.

00:03:47.747 --> 00:03:49.220
Bags of Flamin' Hot Cheetos appeared

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.620
on American store shelves
in the early nineties.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.420
They came to symbolize
the poor eating habits

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:54.460
of America's youth.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.247
The panic intensified with
the rise of social media.

00:03:57.247 --> 00:03:59.860
Flamin' Hot Cheetos, now banned, actually,

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:01.410
in many schools around the country.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:02.900
There were claims that
kids were showing up

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:05.680
at doctor's offices with
heartburn and red stool.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.900
The ban just seems to make the snacks

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:08.733
more tempting.

00:04:08.733 --> 00:04:10.610
Flamin' Hot Cheetos had already permeated

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:11.607
pop culture.

00:04:11.607 --> 00:04:14.780
♪ Cop me some Hot Cheetos
and a Lemonade Brisk ♪

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:17.120
They love the spiciness, the crunch,

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.150
the illicit nature of the snacks.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:21.050
The goal is to eat as many Hot Cheetos

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:23.230
as possible in 10 minutes.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:24.930
Today, YouTube creators unintentionally

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.780
mock the calorie intake concerns
of school administrators

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:28.780
and dieticians.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.080
Here, Matt Stonie takes the 10,000 calorie

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:32.733
Flamin' Hot Cheetos challenge.

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:35.040
Ooh, that burns, the lick.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.820
The snack started appearing in places

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:38.720
its creators never dreamt of.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.970
On Etsy, there are Flamin'
Hot Cheetos earrings,

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:44.290
false eyelashes and
nails, shoes, clothing,

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:45.780
and even household items.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:47.150
I almost bought the leggings.

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:50.250
There was some, there was
some leggings I wanted

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.650
so bad.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.090
But I think they, like,
sold out or something.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:56.830
I'm still hopeful for their future.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:59.040
They're extreme spiciness
is used as a metaphor

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.660
for political convictions

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:02.700
and expressions of individual power.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:05.070
This pin with Flamin' Hot
Cheetos colors and style

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:06.710
features the name Chingona,

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:08.830
Spanish slang for bad-ass woman.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:11.900
The cultural remixing is
also happening with food.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:13.960
It's not just a flavor, it's a memory,

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:15.700
It's a, like, occasion,

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:17.790
you just flashback to all the times you

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:18.840
eat "Flamin' Hot."

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:22.000
We have an, esquite, which
is a corn that we call

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.240
a "Me so Corny."

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:25.990
With the butter, the
mayonnaise, the cotija cheese,

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:27.150
lime chili powder.

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:28.900
And then if you want to get "Flamin' Hot,"

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.840
we put crushed "Flamin' Hot" right on top.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:35.521
And it takes you to nostalgia heaven.

00:05:35.521 --> 00:05:38.390
I'm going to be eating Hot Cheeto sushi.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:41.260
I did make these my own,
not the sushi though,

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:44.120
but like the Hot Cheeto on the sushi.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:45.630
Here, YouTube creator, Wendy,

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:47.400
from the "Wendy's Eating Show" channel,

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.620
is Mexicana, eating a
Japanese dish, and taking part

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:53.445
in a Korean trend on
YouTube called a mukbang,

00:05:53.445 --> 00:05:54.440
a video where someone eats food

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:56.427
and talks to the audience about it.

00:05:56.427 --> 00:05:58.740
You guys want to know why I picked these?

00:05:58.740 --> 00:05:59.980
These sushi rolls?

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:03.800
Because they have cream
cheese in the middle,

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:07.290
and Hot Cheetos and cream
cheese also go together like

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:09.369
peanut butter and jam, so.

00:06:09.369 --> 00:06:11.680
Flamin' Hot Cheetos are uniquely American.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:15.903
They give us the opportunity to engage with cultures that are different from our own, 

00:06:15.903 --> 00:06:20.241
or allow us to define new, surprising, and dynamic ways to be.

00:06:20.241 --> 00:06:26.538
Love comes in all flavors, you know, it's just the different chip.

00:06:26.538 --> 00:06:30.923
The love story that I'm telling is

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:34.928
you can be as different
as you want to be or

00:06:34.928 --> 00:06:36.753
non-traditional as you want to be

00:06:36.753 --> 00:06:40.000
and still make delicious, yummy food.

